In this curve <math>\text{ABCD}</math> are nodes. <math>\text{OA} = e</math> is termed the epoch, being the distance from <math>\text{O}</math> of the first ascending node. <math>\text{AC}</math> is the shortest distance after which the curve begins to repeat itself; this length <math>\lambda</math> is termed the wave-length. The maximum height of the curve <math>\text{HM} = a</math> is the amplitude. If we transfer <math>\text{O}</math> to <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>e = 0</math>, and the curve may be represented by <math>y = a \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} x</math>.

If now the curve moves along unchanged in form in the direction <math>\text{ABC}</math> with uniform velocity <math>\text{U}</math>, the epoch <math>e = \text{OA}</math> at any time <math>t</math> will be <math>\text{U}t</math>, so that the value of <math>y</math> may be represented as
{{block center|<math>y = a \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x - \text{U}t).</math> (16)}}

The velocity perpendicular to the axis of any point on the curve at a fixed distance <math>x</math> from <math>\text{O}</math> is
{{block center|<math>\frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac{2\pi \text{U}a}{\lambda} \cos \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x - \text{U}t).</math> (17)}}

The acceleration perpendicular to the axis is
{{block center|<math>\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} = -\frac{4\pi^2\text{U}^2a}{\lambda^2} \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x - \text{U}t)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= -\frac{4\pi^2\text{U}^2}{\lambda^2} y</math> (18)}}
which is an equation characteristic of simple harmonic motion.

The maximum velocity of a particle in the wave-train is the amplitude of <math>dy/dt</math>. It is, therefore,
{{block center|<math>u_m = 2\pi \text{U}a/\lambda = 2\pi na.</math> (19)}}

